00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:12.080
Volatility often moves inversely to market direction,

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.340
with spikes frequently marking bottoms rather than tops.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:18.360
Today's article from Options Play highlights

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:22.100
how the VIX jumped to 19 around the last FOMC meeting

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:24.720
before reversing to 15 the next day,

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:28.160
A pattern that has historically signaled short-term bottoms.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:33.960
To apply this, investors can monitor the VIX relative to its 20-day average on platforms

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:39.560
like TradingView or Thinkorswim, entering positions only when a spike reverses with volume

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:41.280
confirmation above the average.

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:46.980
This approach worked well after the March 2020 COVID crash when volatility peaked above

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:49.120
80 before equities bottomed.

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:55.520
The risk lies in false reversals during prolonged bear markets where elevated volatility persists.

00:01:56.120 --> 00:02:02.960
Canadian investors can track this in a TFSA by pairing VIX alerts with sector ETFs like XIU.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:08.540
Always require both the volatility reversal and a volume filter before committing capital.

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:14.640
The inverse correlation means market crashes tend to coincide with elevated implied volatility,

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:18.020
while market tops align with low volatility levels.

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:23.480
goals. Imagine you bought a broad market ETF last week after seeing a sharp VIX jump

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:28.940
on news of rate concerns. Your order filled at the market open, but the real mechanics

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:33.960
involve how implied volatility affects option premiums and underlying order flow in the

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:40.780
first 30 minutes. The VIX spike to 19, followed by a drop to 15, shows how fear can create

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:46.180
oversold conditions that reverse quickly when selling exhausts. Most retail investors

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:51.500
monitors assume high volatility means more downside ahead, yet the data shows spikes often

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:56.800
coincide with capitulation. The pro tip is to check the VIX term structure on your broker

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:02.700
platform before entering, looking for contango that signals the spike is temporary. Avoid

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:08.180
the misconception that volatility is just noise by treating it as a timing filter that requires

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:14.140
volume confirmation above the 20-day average to validate any bounce. This filter helps

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:17.520
to distinguish temporary fear from sustained selling pressure.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:21.260
This is a simulated trade for educational purposes only.

